<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Al-Marj</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Barce</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Marj is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a>, north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, on a fertile upland plain inland from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast. It stands near the site of the ancient Greek city of Barca and was known as Barce under Italian rule. The old town was destroyed by an earthquake in 1963, and a new town was built nearby.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-marj.jpg" alt="Al-Marj" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Smiley.toerist · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al_Marj,_Libya.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Marj, in Arabic al-Marj, meaning The Meadows, is a city in northeastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> and the administrative seat of the Marj District. It lies in an upland valley separated from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean Sea</a> by a range of hills, part of the Jebel Akhdar Mountains. It had an estimated population of 85,315 in 2004. Under Italian rule it was linked to Benghazi by the Bengasi-Barce railroad, of which it was the eastern terminal, and today two roads join it to Benghazi, one through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taucheira">Tocra</a> on the Libyan Coastal Highway and the other through Al Abyar.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Most archaeologists place the ancient city of Barca at Marj, although Alexander Graham located it at Tolmeita, ancient Ptolemais. The modern town grew around a Turkish fort built in 1842 and now restored. During the colonial dominance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, from 1913 to 1941, it was called Barce and was developed as an administrative and market centre and a hill resort.</p><p>In the North African campaign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>, the 1st Field Regiment of the Royal Australian Artillery won a battle for the region of Barce on 5 February 1941, and the regiment commemorates it by naming its facilities at Enoggera Barce Lines. From 1942 to 1943 the town was the capital of British-occupied Cyrenaica. A 5.6 earthquake on 21 February 1963 destroyed most of it, killing some 300 people and injuring 500 more. Major rebuilding took place about 5 kilometres from the old site and was completed about 1970.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Marj” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Herodotus relates that around 560 BCE the brothers of King Arcesilaus II of the Kingdom of Cyrene quarrelled with him and founded Barca, on the plain inland from the coast (near modern <b>al-Marj</b>), with the support of local Libyans.</blockquote><a href="/state/barca">Barca</a></li></ul></section>
